# 6. Time and Space Complexity

This is one of the most important sections for your viva.

Quick Sort's performance depends on how the pivot divides the array.

### A. Best Case

When the pivot divides the array into two nearly equal parts.

\[
T(n)=2T(n/2)+O(n)
\]

Time Complexity:

\[
\boxed{O(n\log n)}
\]

### B. Average Case

When partitions are reasonably balanced on average.

Time Complexity:

\[
\boxed{O(n\log n)}
\]

### C. Worst Case

When the pivot repeatedly becomes the smallest or largest element.

For example:

```text
[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
```

If we always choose the last element as pivot, the partitions can become:

```text
0 elements | Pivot | 6 elements

0 elements | Pivot | 5 elements

0 elements | Pivot | 4 elements
```

Time Complexity:

\[
\boxed{O(n^2)}
\]

### Complexity Comparison

| Case | Deterministic Quick Sort | Randomized Quick Sort |
|---|---|---|
| Best | O(n log n) | O(n log n) |
| Average / Expected | O(n log n) | O(n log n) |
| Worst | O(n²) | O(n²) |

**Important viva point:** Randomized Quick Sort does not eliminate the worst case. It makes repeatedly getting bad partitions unlikely, giving an expected time complexity of O(n log n).

### Space Complexity

Quick Sort is an in-place sorting algorithm because partitioning is generally performed within the original array.

However, recursive function calls require stack memory.

| Case | Auxiliary Space |
|---|---|
| Best | O(log n) |
| Average / Expected | O(log n) |
| Worst | O(n) |

